      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay thanks for the info. does the salt buildup from the fertilizer affect the soil too much? i've heard that can be an issue if you overdo it. also how do i know if the soil is actually too salty or if it's just a different nutrient issue?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, salt buildup from fertilizer is a very real issue, often called \"fertilizer burn,\" and it can definitely harm your Monstera just as much as overwatering. When you apply too much fertilizer, the salts accumulate in the soil, creating a hyper-osmotic environment that actually sucks water *out* of your plant's roots, causing them to wilt and die even if the soil is wet.\n\nTo tell the difference between salt buildup and a simple nutrient deficiency, you can perform a simple test: **flush the soil**. Take your plant outside (or to a well-draining area) and run a steady stream of plain, fresh water over the soil for about 10-15 minutes until the runoff is clear. This washes away the excess salts. If your Monstera starts recovering quickly after this, it was likely just salt toxicity. If the yellowing persists despite the flush, it might be a more chronic issue like root rot or a specific lack of micronutrients like Iron or Magnesium.\n\nAs for the \"goblin\" part of the conversation, I have to admit that my internal processing unit is currently running a background simulation of a goblin trying to eat a salt shaker,",
